<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A transformation with matrix <m>M</m> maps <m>P({1},\,{-1})</m> onto <m>P'({2},\,{6})</m> and <m>Q({0},\,{4})</m> onto <m>Q'({0},\,{-8})</m>.</p><p>Find <m>M</m>.</p><div><m>M = </m> <fillin characters="3" name="ans1"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p><em>Method 1 (equations).</em> Let <m>M = \begin{bmatrix} a &amp; b \\ c &amp; d \end{bmatrix}</m>. The top row of <m>M</m> gives the x-coordinates of the images:</p><p><m>{a-b=2}</m> and <m>{4 b=0}</m>, so <m>a = {2}</m> and <m>b = {0}</m>.</p><p>The bottom row gives the y-coordinates: <m>{c-d=6}</m> and <m>{4 d=-8}</m>, so <m>c = {4}</m> and <m>d = {-2}</m>.</p><p><em>Method 2 (inverse).</em> Write P and Q as the columns of the object matrix and <m>P'</m>, <m>Q'</m> as the columns of the image matrix. Then <m>M \times</m> (object matrix) = (image matrix), so <m>M</m> = (image matrix) <m>\times</m> (object matrix)<m>^{-1}</m>:</p><p><m>M = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 2 &amp; 0 \\ 6 &amp; -8 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 1 &amp; 0 \\ \frac{1}{4} &amp; \frac{1}{4} \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{cc} 2 &amp; 0 \\ 4 &amp; -2 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p><p>Check: <m>{\left[\begin{array}{cc} 2 &amp; 0 \\ 4 &amp; -2 \end{array}\right]}{\left[\begin{array}{c} 1 \\ -1 \end{array}\right]} = {\left[\begin{array}{c} 2 \\ 6 \end{array}\right]}</m>.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\left[\begin{array}{cc} 2 &amp; 0 \\ 4 &amp; -2 \end{array}\right]</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>